{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the variances of the two lotteries and preferring the one with the smaller variance. Variance can be computed from the outcomes and probabilities. For Lottery A: outcomes are -6000 (0.1%) and 0 (99.9%). For Lottery B: outcomes are -3000 (0.2%) and 0 (99.8%). The expected value for both lotteries is the same: E[A] = (-6000)(0.001) + (0)(0.999) = -6; E[B] = (-3000)(0.002) + (0)(0.998) = -6. Variance is calculated as E[X²] - (E[X])². For A: E[A²] = (6000²)(0.001) + (0)(0.999) = 36,000,000 × 0.001 = 36,000; Var(A) = 36,000 - (-6)² = 36,000 - 36 = 35,964. For B: E[B²] = (3000²)(0.002) = 9,000,000 × 0.002 = 18,000; Var(B) = 18,000 - 36 = 17,964. Therefore, Var(A) = 35,964 > Var(B) = 17,964. The decision reason would prefer Lottery B due to its smaller variance. The reason is applicable to the decision problem.",
